Op-point  THE DIAGNOSIS IS IN ONE DEVICE:
            XMP3  vds 0.0903  vdsat 0.1102 -> 0.1275   vov = -0.037  OUT OF SAT
            XMP3  gds 20.00 -> 636.46 uS   gm/gds 74.87 -> 2.03
            XMN5  vds 1.1846 -> 1.7097     (vout railed toward VDD)
          I_tail 28.47 -> 42.49 ; I_XMP1 14.24 -> 21.25
Reasoning Predicted gm1 x1.5 -> UGBW x1.5 at restored Cc, PM roughly held.
          Expected ~17.9 MHz.
Next      PREDICTION WRONG, and the mechanism is a real constraint this design
          has that nothing had yet written down:
            XMP3's gate is net5 and XMP1/XMP2's gate is net4, and in balance the
            two nodes sit at the SAME DC level -- so XMP3 sees the same Vgs as
            the load mirror, and therefore
                I_XMP3 = I_XMP1 * (W/L)_XMP3/(W/L)_XMP1 = I_XMP1 * 120/14.4
                       = 8.333 * I_tail/2
            The output only sits mid-rail when XMN5 sinks exactly that. Raising
            the tail 1.5x demands 1.5x from XMN5 too; leaving MN5_M at 6 left
            XMP3 driving 177 uA into a 107 uA sink, so vout railed high and the
            stage-2 pfet fell into triode -- taking 25 dB of gain with it.
          So the tail is NOT independently tunable: I_tail and I_XMN5 must move
          TOGETHER, in the ratio the XMP3/XMP1 width ratio fixes. To rerun this
          properly MN5_M would need ~10, costing ~0.53 mW -- 40% more power than
          iteration 4 already achieves. REJECTED on the minimum-power objective.
          Reverting the tail and going back to the compensation network instead:
          shrink Cc for UGBW and RE-TUNE R0 for the new Cc (xpd1 says they move
          together), which buys the harder bar for zero current.

